  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.6 z^2) / 2, drawn at half its value as height. x (left to right) is the steep direction: steps overshoot when learning rate > 0.42 and swings grow when it is above 0.83.",
    "Each step = -learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is random, centred on zero, with spread 1.5 × sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at 64. It comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 5 times their true length, one scale for both. Mini-batch step is the way the ball moves next; Full-data step is -learning rate × the true slope.",
    "A run finishes after 40 steps. It is diverged when the loss goes above 6. A ball off the bowl is drawn at the rim, and the Loss readout keeps its true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus one point per step, up to 41 points. The ghost keeps the previous run's path."
  ]
}